  <section class="topic-section wrap">
    <h2>What does this mean for a supplier or consultant?</h2>
    <p><strong>Research interpretation:</strong> use the amended five-plus-five configuration when describing this permitted cohort. Preserve the earlier six-plus-six description as history. Combining both versions as separate equipment would overstate the documented fleet.</p>
    <p>The longer LM2500 permitted-hours envelope changes the authorization described by the review. It does not establish annual run hours, availability, capacity factor or service demand. Those commercial and operating assumptions need their own evidence.</p>
    <p>The legal-entity and site-name match is useful for following later filings: the permit uses Abilene DC 1 / Longhorn, while the project is commonly discussed as Crusoe Stargate Abilene. The record does not establish an unawarded order or identify a supplier opportunity that is currently available.</p>
    <h2>What remains unknown?</h2>
    <ul><li>Current installed and operating configuration across the complete campus.</li><li>Measured net generation and power delivered to computing at a defined time.</li><li>The exact first day of behind-the-meter generation.</li><li>Project-specific heat rate, delivered gas price, installed cost and service economics.</li><li>Current procurement, construction and service packages available to outside suppliers.</li></ul>
    <h2>What would the next research check cover?</h2>
    <ol><li>Look for later records under the matched entity, site, registration and permit identifiers; record the date and result of the check.</li><li>Compare the scope of any later filing with the existing cohort before treating it as additional equipment.</li><li>Seek dated commissioning or operating evidence for claims about current configuration or output.</li><li>Validate commercial participants and available scope directly before qualifying a sales opportunity.</li></ol>
    <p>These are proposed follow-up checks, not claims that a new filing or contract exists. Automated TCEQ retrieval currently has access limits; a pilot must agree a workable source-review route rather than promise uninterrupted coverage.</p>
  </section>
